      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62331,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 375,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Ovary</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 463,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 376,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62332,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 376,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Abdominal pain, abdominal mass, and menstrual disorder are typical presenting features. Premenarchal children may present with precocious pseudopuberty. Serum LDH, &beta;-hCG, or AFP can be elevated, depending on what components are present in the tumour.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 464,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 377,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62333,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 377,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These account for 10&ndash;20% of all malignant germ cell tumours. They occur mainly in children and young women. Rare cases occur in patients with abnormal karyotypes, sometimes arising in a gonadoblastoma {185553; 7814189; 16547466; 28372870; 29183227}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 465,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 378,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62334,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 378,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 466,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 379,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62335,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 379,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Many tumours exhibit a gain of 12p or isochromosome 12p {16547466}. Some tumours arise in a gonadoblastoma in patients with a normal or abnormal karyotype {29183227}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 473,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 386,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62336,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 386,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These are large tumours, averaging 15&nbsp;cm, with solid and cystic areas. The appearance depends on the elements that are present. Dysgerminoma is tan and fleshy. Yolk sac tumour areas contain small cysts and foci of necrosis. A choriocarcinoma component is haemorrhagic and necrotic. Embryonal carcinoma contains zones of necrosis. Immature teratoma may contain gritty areas of bone or cartilage.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 467,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 380,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62337,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 380,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most tumours contain two or more malignant germ cell components; the rest contain three or more components. Specific quantitative criteria have not been established for the minimal amount of a second component for a case to qualify as a mixed germ cell tumour. However, immature teratomas containing a focus of yolk sac tumour or embryonal carcinoma measuring &gt;&nbsp;3&nbsp;mm have been classified as mixed germ cell tumours {7814189}. The most common combination is dysgerminoma and yolk sac tumour; other components may include embryonal carcinoma, choriocarcinoma, and immature teratoma. The morphological and immunohistochemical features of each individual component are identical to those of their counterparts in pure tumours {185553}. The components are often intimately admixed but may be present in separate areas of the mass.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 468,
    "bookid": 34,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 381,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 62338,
        "bookId": 34,
        "chapterId": 92,
        "headingId": 381,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2020-05-13T17:53:30"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 469,
